{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of S5 at head d683e7cbbd, tree bc4168d1, for request de685cba and review request dd59254b. Verdict: APPROVED, for local use, with one point for the planner before any hosted deployment.\n\nThe head adds 11 paths to main ed321125: a static `work-on` prompt, the guide `examples/repo-in-ai/agents.md`, `Mcp-Name` binding for `prompts/get`, its seal, and tests. No lane act, signer or store is added, and nothing under the kernel changes.\n\n## Your five points\n\n1. No focused claim rests only on older commits that matters here. The gate at a72eed59 reran every owner, and the plan's four witnesses are assertions in those owners. The note's account of the focused runs stays history. From a72eed59 to this head, only the note and the manifest change.\n\n2. Confirmed. `git diff main d683e7cb -- packages/mcp/src/http.ts` holds only the `Mcp-Name` change, two changed lines, and main already holds the lost-abort repair. The remerge diff of 110a0d56 resolves one conflict, the `http.ts` seal, to a new literal. `security-entry-points.test.mjs` passes 10 of 10 with it.\n\n3. Yes, the fresh-session case resumes from record reads alone. I read it for anything crossing over. A new `McpService`, signer wrapper, custody store and read caches are built per session. `resume` takes only the session and derives the lane from `room`, and the commitment, block, ask, decision and terms from reads. The saved config is public and drops `inbox`. The only things shared are the test key, the public config and the native room state, which is what the record is. `expect(agent.signed).toEqual([])` holds until the first `accept`, and the Membership head stays unchanged, so it didn't re-enrol.\n\n4. Yes, the timed-out wait is a fair witness of \"a timed-out wait writes nothing\". It compares the lane summary and the native entry count, no intent signed, the custody records unchanged, and no act POST. One small gap: its regex looks for `/acts` or `/prepare`, and the real route is `/preparations`, while `settle` isn't named at all. The summary and entry comparison catches any accepted act anyway, so I don't ask for a change. The note states the clock's limit correctly.\n\n5. The remaining text instructs nothing an agent should not do. It says that a prompt is guidance and not authority, tells the agent not to approve or decide for a person, to block only its own commitment, to settle rather than re-sign, and that no handover happens. `goal` enters through `JSON.stringify` and is bounded. One omission: the prompt sends an agent to read issue and question text that other members wrote, and neither it nor `INSTRUCTIONS` says that room text is data and grants nothing. \"A prompt is guidance, not authority\" is about the prompt itself. One sentence would close that, and I'd add it when this text next changes.\n\n## For the planner: the prompt doesn't work on the hosted catalogue as it stands\n\nThis surface reaches hosted MCP at your next deployment. `HOSTED_TOOL_OMISSIONS` removes `step`, `text` and `record`, and `steps` gives one line per entry with no hash. `accept` and `release-block` need full FactRefs for `termsAt` and the decide entry. The prompt says \"inspect, items and step\", and the guide says \"Use `step` to obtain its full fact\". By my reading, a hosted agent has no general way to get those hashes, and it also can't read a question's full text. I found no `MCP_TOOL_ADDITIONS` in the wrangler files I searched, and no hosted run exists, so this comes from reading only.\n\nThe guide says that it makes no hosted-agent claim, and no deployment happens before J4 closes, so this doesn't block landing. Before you deploy this surface, choose one: add `step` and `text` to the hosted set, withhold the prompt on hosted, or state on the hosted surface that the loop isn't supported there. This is the same gap as point 5 in my BO1 review.\n\n## What I also checked\n\n- The prompt reads no room. `wire.test.ts` asserts no scope request, through the real client in both revisions, and it passes 14 of 14 when I run it. Prompt arguments outside `goal` and goals over 4096 bytes refuse.\n- `terms-differ` is witnessed on the native path, with the item unchanged after the refusal. One agent's block with the other's accepted Change proposal is witnessed too. That proposal is in a different lane, so the witness shows no lane-wide stop, which is what the plan asks.\n- The gate's `test.log` shows Node 98, Counting 56, Plain 3,205 with one skip and Native 890 with two skips. The earlier gate that failed on the prompt's repository path stays failed in its own directory.\n- The evidence verifies. All 21 manifest rows match, the manifest has digest d6c454ab, the directory has 200 files, its `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest dd7cbda7, and no file is writable.\n- `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main ed321125 gives tree bc4168d1. `check-kernel-boundary` checks 127 sources with 0 refusals, `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, `check-commit-messages` checks 7 with 0 flagged, `check-doc-links` on an export finds 775 links with 0 broken, and `git diff --check` is clean. The worktree stayed clean.\n\nI ran no independent second read, and I did not run the native owners again.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. `docs/mcp.md` doesn't name the prompt. The guide is under `examples/`, so a reader of the tool page won't find it.\n2. Commit history holds three commits with the same subject, \"Add a same-key agent wait and resume prompt\". The check passes them, and they're harmless.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe prompt composes existing tools, and the wire change is two switch arms in each revision. Approved, with the hosted point above.\n\n## Security\n\nA static prompt adds no authority, and `Mcp-Name` binding matches the rule for tools. Approved.\n\n## Simplification\n\nOne file of prompt text and one guide. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The 